1
00:00:14,828 --> 00:00:15,829
[SPEAKER_02]: I think I have a question.

2
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:33,837
[SPEAKER_02]: Is seclusion, is that an activity or is that a result of practice?

3
00:00:35,758 --> 00:00:37,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Both, depending how you look at it.

4
00:00:38,379 --> 00:00:40,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it has to start as an activity.

5
00:00:41,161 --> 00:00:43,662
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to start something you actively seek.

6
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:50,821
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you practice rightly, your mind becomes secluded from unwholesome things, and that's then the result.

7
00:00:51,481 --> 00:00:57,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha did say that the true seclusion is when mind is free from greed, aversion, delusion, not when one is away from people.

8
00:00:58,903 --> 00:01:05,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Although he might be alone, he still dwells in company of sensual desire and unwholesome states.

9
00:01:05,404 --> 00:01:10,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But that resulting seclusion would not just come

10
00:01:11,506 --> 00:01:18,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Magically, if you have not... if you can't even, like, live alone, be by yourself, at least for some time of the day.

11
00:01:18,770 --> 00:01:23,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have to do it as a practice, that can then lead to the actual seclusion.

12
00:01:28,315 --> 00:01:29,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's kind of...

13
00:01:30,862 --> 00:01:42,226
[SPEAKER_01]: A balance of both, in a sense, because sometimes people try to justify non-physical seclusion, would say, well, see, the true seclusion is just being free from unwholesome states.

14
00:01:42,986 --> 00:01:47,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, doesn't matter if I just spend my days with people, or all day long.

15
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:55,031
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you have the other group of people who just hates all the people and just wants to be alone because that's an easier mode of existence.

16
00:01:55,572 --> 00:01:59,192
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they just quote the suttas of the activity aspect of seclusion.

17
00:01:59,292 --> 00:02:02,553
[SPEAKER_01]: But that aspect in the gradual training comes much later.

18
00:02:02,673 --> 00:02:03,773
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't start with it.

19
00:02:03,813 --> 00:02:06,254
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're starting with it, there's usually something else there.

20
00:02:07,094 --> 00:02:10,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Some other kind of material motive you have.

21
00:02:11,743 --> 00:02:19,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so basically, the Buddhist teaching is actually very easily wrongly grasped to actually enforce your version.

22
00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:22,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Easy, easy, of course.

23
00:02:22,192 --> 00:02:23,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything, anything.

24
00:02:23,073 --> 00:02:30,219
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha himself giving instruction, if you don't attend to it rightly, you'll just use it for your own sake of your own wrong views, yeah.

25
00:02:32,761 --> 00:02:33,682
[SPEAKER_05]: It's interesting in the

26
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:48,425
[SPEAKER_05]: Sutta is like, to be content in solitude or to delight in seclusion, is considered quite a high achievement.

27
00:02:48,465 --> 00:02:49,605
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, work.

28
00:02:49,685 --> 00:02:51,446
[SPEAKER_05]: It's really work just to get there.

29
00:02:51,546 --> 00:02:51,826
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

30
00:02:54,987 --> 00:03:04,917
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, one thing to consider in regard to that is like, you know, if for a monk at that time, dwelling in solitude wouldn't necessarily mean quite the same thing as it would today.

31
00:03:05,557 --> 00:03:15,327
[SPEAKER_05]: As in, it would not just be, okay, I'm in a room on my own, but like, whenever I want, I can contact somebody or I can, you know, access some distraction or whatever.

32
00:03:15,827 --> 00:03:18,750
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, really, I'm in my own on a forest and

33
00:03:20,191 --> 00:03:23,214
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if I want to, there is nobody around.

34
00:03:23,234 --> 00:03:30,199
[SPEAKER_05]: So it's like, to be content within that environment is quite a high achievement.

35
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:31,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

36
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:40,907
[SPEAKER_05]: So it's not just like, oh, I'll go on my own and I can do whatever I want and, you know, go back and contact people or, you know, whatever I want.

37
00:03:42,168 --> 00:03:44,250
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like you have to kind of put yourself into that

38
00:03:45,968 --> 00:04:00,856
[SPEAKER_05]: My mental state, even if you're not in this, that you can't, you don't have to kind of, I don't know, I'll go up on a mountain and forsake everything, but like into that, you know, that mental state of, okay, isolation and complete isolation.

39
00:04:01,576 --> 00:04:06,019
[SPEAKER_05]: Ultimately, I mean, that's obviously then a training that you have to work towards.

40
00:04:06,659 --> 00:04:09,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Difficult thing to do, to delight in solitude.

41
00:04:10,361 --> 00:04:13,663
[SPEAKER_01]: But once there's a light and solitude, the goal is quickly achieved.

42
00:04:14,657 --> 00:04:15,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the discourse.

43
00:04:17,199 --> 00:04:23,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because it's the whole, the whole gradual training we talk about.

44
00:04:23,964 --> 00:04:24,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it leads to.

45
00:04:26,086 --> 00:04:27,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Delighting solitude.

46
00:04:27,807 --> 00:04:28,347
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's like...

47
00:04:28,507 --> 00:04:30,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Preferring it.

48
00:04:30,229 --> 00:04:37,814
[SPEAKER_05]: Just to be literally alone, even without anything to do with it, like without anything... Yeah, yeah, of course, yeah, yeah.

49
00:04:38,054 --> 00:04:40,076
[SPEAKER_01]: The non-activity, the true non-activity, yeah.

50
00:04:41,965 --> 00:04:48,826
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to be alone with yourself, which is the most... it's... and it's also like the worst punishment you can give to somebody in the world.

51
00:04:49,006 --> 00:04:50,767
[SPEAKER_05]: You put them in solitary confinement.

52
00:04:51,147 --> 00:04:57,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Without any visible activity they can engage with, and it just... it becomes unbearable.

53
00:04:57,988 --> 00:04:59,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Confining.

54
00:04:59,168 --> 00:05:00,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Confining.

55
00:05:02,789 --> 00:05:07,410
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have others around, there's... there's an opportunity for...

56
00:05:08,768 --> 00:05:09,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

57
00:05:09,809 --> 00:05:10,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction.

58
00:05:10,930 --> 00:05:11,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

59
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:23,500
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, like, you do have to emphasize that it's not just something, okay, so I'll just train for... You train by keeping the precepts, guarding the sense doors, don't give your eyes to unwholesome states, certainly don't act out of them.

60
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:29,024
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the light and solitude become, as a result of that, that then you can actively develop further.

61
00:05:30,125 --> 00:05:31,506
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're trying to jump the queue,

62
00:05:32,742 --> 00:05:41,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Before, you have been established in the right view, established in virtue, all those things that the Buddha lists to that monk they want to go in solitude with, who wasn't ready.

63
00:05:41,985 --> 00:05:43,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you won't be ready.

64
00:05:43,545 --> 00:05:50,327
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be going for wrong reasons and just dwell there either with mind infested with lust or hatred or whatever else bothers you.

65
00:05:53,308 --> 00:05:54,588
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll fail, in that sense.

66
00:05:56,069 --> 00:06:00,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, what can easily happen... See, like, people

67
00:06:03,293 --> 00:06:10,306
[SPEAKER_01]: It can kind of play into that natural tendency to just avoid things that bother me.

68
00:06:17,905 --> 00:06:21,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because, you know, the Buddha said seclusion is the atman's goal.

69
00:06:21,826 --> 00:06:33,929
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I feel justified then, in just sitting in my kuti, not seeing, not talking to anybody, not doing anything, because the slightest act will just disturb my mind, make it filled with ill-will, flooded with passion, and so on.

70
00:06:35,149 --> 00:06:41,411
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, your seclusion becomes your coping mechanism, whereby you're super, hyper, extra sensitive.

71
00:06:42,231 --> 00:06:45,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Even before, when you were a layman, you were not that sensitive.

72
00:06:45,492 --> 00:06:46,852
[SPEAKER_01]: You could actually endure more.

73
00:06:47,987 --> 00:06:50,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, like, the ticking clock is driving me nuts.

74
00:06:51,209 --> 00:06:53,170
[SPEAKER_01]: The blow of wind, the water, river.

75
00:06:53,210 --> 00:06:54,630
[SPEAKER_01]: I just can't bear this noise.

76
00:06:54,690 --> 00:06:56,031
[SPEAKER_01]: My meditation is suffering.

77
00:06:56,071 --> 00:06:56,431
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't...

78
00:06:56,771 --> 00:06:57,632
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to change a kuti.

79
00:06:57,652 --> 00:06:58,592
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to change a monastery.

80
00:06:58,612 --> 00:06:59,473
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to change a country.

81
00:06:59,493 --> 00:07:00,233
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to change a place.

82
00:07:00,273 --> 00:07:00,933
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to change a place.

83
00:07:01,354 --> 00:07:04,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Quiet, quiet, earplugs, soundproof room.

84
00:07:04,935 --> 00:07:06,036
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not seclusion.

85
00:07:07,396 --> 00:07:09,778
[SPEAKER_01]: That's absolute, complete kind of...

86
00:07:13,066 --> 00:07:15,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, extreme way, perversion of avoidance.

87
00:07:15,787 --> 00:07:21,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Avoiding things that bother you, because your view still thinks the bother comes from those things.

88
00:07:23,129 --> 00:07:28,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you haven't even abandoned the mundane wrong views of where the suffering comes from.

89
00:07:28,050 --> 00:07:32,131
[SPEAKER_01]: While you sit in your padded cell, where nothing can come through, when you are at peace.

90
00:07:33,392 --> 00:07:34,292
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the peace.

91
00:07:34,952 --> 00:07:39,594
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean... Highway robbers chopping your limbs, your mind doesn't move.

92
00:07:39,894 --> 00:07:41,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the peace, yeah.

93
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:55,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Elements, enduring elements, discomfort, I mean, you can read, you like read the the khandhaka stories around, you know, vinaya and then how the monks lived, the engagement and the exposure and the people and stuff, and it's like...

94
00:07:57,099 --> 00:08:01,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of it was quite hectic, but there were arahants with supernatural powers and stuff like that.

95
00:08:02,328 --> 00:08:08,671
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, okay, so there is probably some balance there, that today's average Buddhist would not necessarily see.

96
00:08:08,711 --> 00:08:14,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, okay, so let me then start in the right order, as the Buddha described in the gradual training.

97
00:08:15,754 --> 00:08:26,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you know, simplify your life, don't sit amidst of a loud crowd, avoid obviously unwholesome environment, but don't get too precious about it, because it's going to go too much the other way.

98
00:08:26,739 --> 00:08:32,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, you start enjoying seclusion, because you start feeding the bother even more.

99
00:08:33,372 --> 00:08:34,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know how to deal with it.

100
00:08:35,973 --> 00:08:46,717
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like the dog tied around the post tries to get himself free and ends up being even more tied to the post, even shorter leash.

101
00:08:51,098 --> 00:08:58,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ultimately, the bother, the disturbances, it doesn't come from the world.

102
00:08:58,141 --> 00:09:00,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes from that craving, comes from that resistance.

103
00:09:01,981 --> 00:09:03,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, same goes with the company.

104
00:09:05,023 --> 00:09:16,789
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the mind moves pleasantly, unpleasantly towards the company, it's all because of your own resistance towards it, not because of that person or these people and so on.

105
00:09:18,009 --> 00:09:25,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where the true solitude is, when that has been calmed and tamed and extinguished, that movement, that craving, that resistance.

106
00:09:27,347 --> 00:09:32,391
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha said... Well, the suttas say, Arahant, he's as in private, so in public.

107
00:09:32,871 --> 00:09:34,012
[SPEAKER_01]: As in public, so in private.

108
00:09:34,132 --> 00:09:34,752
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no difference.

109
00:09:35,453 --> 00:09:36,814
[SPEAKER_01]: His behavior does not change.

110
00:09:36,854 --> 00:09:38,535
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no different reference point to that.

111
00:09:40,436 --> 00:09:48,902
[SPEAKER_01]: And like the Buddha said, the Tāgata, or he's, it is, whether he's assembly of the gods, or villages, one person, thousand, nothing changes in terms of his mental composure.

112
00:09:51,764 --> 00:09:52,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the true solitude.

113
00:09:55,900 --> 00:10:02,368
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's more in regard to how jhānas are usually described, basically seclusion from the wholesome states.

114
00:10:02,488 --> 00:10:02,648
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

115
00:10:02,948 --> 00:10:10,157
[SPEAKER_04]: But to see what those are, it's beneficial to go into physical seclusion.

116
00:10:10,337 --> 00:10:14,702
[SPEAKER_04]: So, or kind of, how to say, decrease the intensity of

117
00:10:16,763 --> 00:10:19,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Things from the world, so that you can see these things.

118
00:10:19,845 --> 00:10:36,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And the step, the necessity of going into physical seclusion, that we ourselves talked about in the past many times, comes after you have been taming your senses properly, thoroughly, not giving rise to a wholesome state, which comes after you've been thoroughly established in virtue and precepts.

119
00:10:37,334 --> 00:10:42,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like over a weekend or something, and now you're going to go into seclusion, so that by the end of the week you enter jhāna.

120
00:10:44,193 --> 00:10:50,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, becoming established in precepts, well, how many years have you spent not being established in precepts?

121
00:10:50,857 --> 00:10:57,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, divided by half, depending on your effort, give or take.

122
00:10:57,500 --> 00:10:58,821
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the previous line.

123
00:11:00,802 --> 00:11:03,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's going to take a while for that mind to tame.

124
00:11:04,464 --> 00:11:05,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, you're going to have

125
00:11:06,974 --> 00:11:12,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Minor releases, so to speak, insights and so on, but it's not a race, really.

126
00:11:13,061 --> 00:11:14,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, I'll just do this.

127
00:11:14,703 --> 00:11:19,268
[SPEAKER_01]: If it is, it's because you're still acting out of your craving.

128
00:11:19,308 --> 00:11:23,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether I get it or not, it's still a preferable mode of existence.

129
00:11:24,572 --> 00:11:31,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Taming and sense restraint is factually better, even according to your own sensual values.

130
00:11:31,955 --> 00:11:35,717
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason people turn to sensuality is because it hurts if they don't.

131
00:11:36,877 --> 00:11:42,420
